-- Add two authored distractors to eight MCQs per concept. Keep existing IDs,
-- answers, explanations, attempts, and sequence questions unchanged.
UPDATE questions q
SET options = q.options || jsonb_build_object('E', seed.extra_e, 'F', seed.extra_f),
    display_option_count = 4
FROM (VALUES
    ('A running process uses its entire time quantum without finishing. If it remains runnable, which state does preemption normally move it to?',
     'Suspended until a disk interrupt occurs', 'Waiting until a new copy of its program is loaded'),
    ('Which action normally requires a system call rather than only ordinary user-mode instructions?',
     'Assigning a value to a local variable already in mapped memory', 'Jumping to another instruction in the same user function'),
    ('Why does a CPU distinguish user mode from kernel mode?',
     'To assign every process a fixed scheduling priority for its lifetime', 'To require a kernel transition for every arithmetic instruction'),
    ('On UNIX, a child has terminated but its parent has not yet collected its exit status. What is this child commonly called?',
     'A suspended process waiting to resume its instructions', 'An orphan process that is necessarily still running'),
    ('Which UNIX operation can a parent use to collect a terminated child process exit status?',
     'exec, which replaces the parent image with the child exit status', 'fork, which returns the exit status of the previously created child'),
    ('What is the purpose of a process identifier (PID)?',
     'To identify the physical frame containing the process first instruction', 'To specify the position of a process in the ready queue'),
    ('A timer interrupt lets the OS regain control while a user process is computing. What capability does this support?',
     'Scheduling only when a process voluntarily makes a blocking call', 'Guaranteeing that the interrupted process has completed its current CPU burst'),
    ('How can two processes executing the same program differ?',
     'They must have different instruction sets even on the same CPU', 'They must have identical private data whenever their executable files match'),
    ('A program has four independent CPU-bound worker threads but only one CPU core. Which expectation is justified?',
     'Four threads guarantee a twofold CPU speedup even without I/O waits', 'Context switching lets the core execute all four instruction streams at the same instant'),
    ('What does thread-local storage provide?',
     'A separate address space for each thread containing no shared process memory', 'A single shared variable whose value automatically rotates among threads'),
    ('In a one-to-one threading model, what is the relationship between user threads and kernel threads?',
     'Each user thread must map to a different physical CPU core', 'One user thread is permanently paired with an entire process rather than a kernel thread'),
    ('In a many-to-one model, a user thread makes a blocking system call that blocks its sole underlying kernel thread. What can happen to its peer user threads?',
     'The kernel automatically creates a new schedulable kernel thread for every peer', 'User-level scheduling can execute peers on the CPU while their only kernel thread remains blocked'),
    ('Why might a server use a bounded worker-thread pool instead of creating an unbounded number of threads?',
     'To guarantee that every arriving request immediately gets a dedicated thread', 'To prevent any task from sharing memory with another task'),
    ('A parent thread creates a worker and immediately joins it before creating the next worker. What is the likely effect on overlap between workers?',
     'Each join only waits for the worker to start, so all workers overlap', 'The completed worker keeps executing concurrently after join returns'),
    ('A worker exits while another thread still holds a pointer to an object on that worker stack. What is the concern?',
     'Holding the pointer guarantees that the stack object remains alive', 'Joining the worker automatically moves all of its local objects into the heap'),
    ('A computation is 50 percent inherently serial and 50 percent perfectly parallelizable. Ignoring overhead, what is its maximum speedup with arbitrarily many processors?',
     '1.5 times', '100 times'),
    ('Three jobs arrive together and run nonpreemptively in FCFS order with CPU bursts 4, 3, and 2. Ignoring overhead, how long does the third wait before starting?',
     '5 time units', '3 time units'),
    ('Jobs A, B, and C are ready at time zero with known CPU bursts 6, 2, and 4. Which order does nonpreemptive shortest-job-first choose?',
     'B, A, C', 'C, B, A'),
    ('Round-robin uses a quantum of 2. A and B arrive at time zero in that order with bursts 3 and 2. Ignoring overhead, when does B finish?',
     'Time 1', 'Time 6'),
    ('If a round-robin quantum exceeds every ready job CPU burst and no new jobs arrive, what behavior does it resemble?',
     'Priority scheduling that always puts the shortest remaining burst first', 'Alternating between jobs after every unit of execution regardless of the quantum'),
    ('A system completes 20 jobs in 5 seconds. What is its throughput over that interval?',
     '5 jobs per second', '15 jobs per second'),
    ('Which scheduling measure most directly captures how soon an interactive task first receives CPU service after arrival?',
     'Turnaround time, measured until the task finishes', 'Total CPU execution time accumulated over the whole task'),
    ('A low-priority thread holds a mutex needed by a high-priority thread, while a medium-priority thread keeps preempting the lock holder. What is this called?',
     'A circular wait that necessarily deadlocks all three threads', 'Priority inheritance already resolving the inversion'),
    ('How can priority inheritance help when a high-priority thread is blocked on a mutex held by a lower-priority thread?',
     'By raising only the waiting thread priority while leaving the lock holder preempted', 'By transferring the mutex immediately to the waiter before the holder finishes its protected update'),
    ('A UNIX pipe has no buffered data and every write descriptor has been closed. What does a read normally return?',
     'It must wait forever for a writer that has already closed', 'The last message again, because closing a pipe resets its read position'),
    ('A reader waits for EOF on a pipe, but another process accidentally keeps an unused write descriptor open. What can happen after the buffer is drained?',
     'A read returns EOF as soon as the buffer is empty regardless of open writers', 'A read synthesizes a final newline to mark the missing close'),
    ('An application sends two logical records over a TCP connection. What must the receiver account for?',
     'A successful receive always returns all bytes currently in the application record', 'A TCP packet boundary is guaranteed to match the application record boundary'),
    ('A nonblocking receive finds no available data on an otherwise open connection. What should the application generally do?',
     'Treat the would-block result as successful EOF', 'Discard the pending request because temporary lack of data means its reply cannot arrive'),
    ('Two processes share a memory region but map it at different virtual addresses. Why is storing a raw pointer from one process in the region problematic?',
     'The pointer automatically contains a portable offset from the shared region start', 'Mapping the same region forces every stored virtual address to become valid in both processes'),
    ('What is a benefit of length-prefixed message framing over a byte stream?',
     'It eliminates the need to check for a truncated payload', 'It guarantees a peer cannot advertise a length larger than available memory'),
    ('A request is processed, but its reply is lost. The client retries. What helps prevent the operation from being performed twice?',
     'Generating a new request ID each time a reply is missing', 'Adding a delay before retrying without tracking previously handled requests'),
    ('A receiver reads only part of a length-prefixed payload before the connection closes. What should it conclude?',
     'Connection close makes the incomplete payload a valid shorter record automatically', 'The length prefix may be ignored because any received prefix of the payload is complete'),
    ('A producer changes a condition predicate while holding the same mutex used by a waiting consumer. Why does this shared locking discipline matter?',
     'It allows consumers to skip checking the predicate after every wakeup', 'It guarantees that one notification makes the predicate permanently true'),
    ('A semaphore tracks occupied slots in an initially empty bounded buffer. What should its initial count be?',
     'The full buffer capacity', 'One, regardless of capacity or initial contents'),
    ('Why is disabling interrupts on only one CPU generally insufficient to protect shared data from other CPUs?',
     'Disabling local interrupts automatically turns every shared load into an atomic read-modify-write', 'Local interrupt masking is automatically propagated to all other cores'),
    ('Several readers can safely inspect a structure concurrently, but updates must exclude readers and other writers. Which abstraction matches this requirement?',
     'A barrier used once at program startup with all later accesses unprotected', 'Separate unrelated mutexes for readers and writers with no shared coordination'),
    ('What is an atomic compare-and-swap operation intended to do?',
     'Read a value now and guarantee a later ordinary store cannot race with another thread', 'Unconditionally exchange two unrelated objects using a sequence of unprotected loads and stores'),
    ('Why is repeatedly sleeping for a fixed interval a poor substitute for locking shared mutable data?',
     'A long enough sleep automatically establishes ownership of the shared object', 'Two threads with different sleep durations can never overlap a read-modify-write operation'),
    ('A thread calls a nonrecursive mutex lock operation twice without unlocking it. What risk does this create?',
     'The second call automatically turns every nonrecursive mutex into a recursive mutex', 'The mutex always interprets the second lock as an unlock'),
    ('What does fairness in a lock acquisition policy aim to reduce?',
     'Circular wait in every possible multi-lock program, regardless of acquisition order', 'The need to release locks after completing protected work'),
    ('A thread holding lock A tries to acquire lock B using try-lock. On failure it releases A before retrying. Which deadlock condition does this protocol try to avoid?',
     'Mutual exclusion, by allowing both threads to hold the same exclusive lock', 'No preemption, by forcibly taking B from its current owner'),
    ('Why is a timeout alone not proof that a deadlock has occurred?',
     'A timeout proves every requested resource is permanently unavailable', 'A timeout directly identifies the complete wait-for cycle without inspecting dependencies'),
    ('With multiple instances of a resource type, a resource-allocation graph contains a cycle. What follows from the cycle alone?',
     'The cycle proves deadlock even if an additional instance can satisfy a waiting request', 'Multiple instances make deadlock impossible in every allocation'),
    ('Two processes each need at most two identical resources to complete. Three instances exist, and each process currently holds one. Why is this state safe?',
     'Both processes must obtain their second instance simultaneously to make any progress', 'A safe state requires every process maximum need to be satisfied at the same instant'),
    ('Two processes each hold one of two identical resource instances and each waits for one more, retaining what it holds. With no preemption or outside release, what is the result?',
     'Round-robin CPU scheduling alone supplies the additional resource each process needs', 'A safe completion sequence exists because each process already owns half its maximum need'),
    ('What distinguishes deadlock detection from deadlock avoidance?',
     'Detection rejects every unsafe allocation in advance using declared maximum needs', 'Avoidance and detection both guarantee that no process ever waits for resources'),
    ('A system chooses to recover from a deadlock by terminating one participating process. Why might this help?',
     'Terminating any process necessarily restores all partially modified shared data to its previous state', 'Termination breaks the cycle even if none of the victim resources can be reclaimed'),
    ('Why can resource preemption be difficult for deadlock recovery when the resource is a printer halfway through a document?',
     'Saving the process CPU registers automatically rolls back all printed pages', 'A printer can always resume a different document at the same physical position without affecting output'),
    ('A page size is 4096 bytes. How many bits of a byte-addressed virtual address specify the offset within a page?',
     '10 bits', '13 bits'),
    ('Virtual page 3 maps to physical frame 9, and the page size is 1024 bytes. What physical byte address corresponds to offset 100 in that page?',
     '9216', '12388'),
    ('A TLB lookup misses, but the referenced page is present in RAM with a valid page-table entry. What follows?',
     'A TLB miss proves the page-table entry is invalid even when the page is resident', 'The CPU can use the virtual page number as the physical frame number without checking the mapping'),
    ('What does copy-on-write allow after two processes initially share a memory page?',
     'A writer always changes the other process private view of the page too', 'Copying is postponed until a read, while writes never require a private version'),
    ('What does a page protection setting that denies writes help the OS enforce?',
     'Preventing all reads from the page even when read permission is present', 'Guaranteeing the page can never be evicted from RAM'),
    ('Which page would least-recently-used replacement choose among resident pages last accessed at times 4, 9, and 12?',
     'The page with the fewest total accesses, regardless of these last-access times', 'The page loaded earliest, regardless of how recently it was accessed'),
    ('Why can multi-level page tables save memory for a sparse virtual address space?',
     'They allocate every lower-level table even for completely unmapped ranges', 'They store one permanent physical frame for every possible virtual page'),
    ('A process repeatedly accesses a small cluster of nearby memory locations. Which property does this illustrate?',
     'Temporal locality alone, defined as accessing different nearby addresses without reusing any location', 'External fragmentation, defined as repeatedly accessing adjacent addresses')
) AS seed(prompt, extra_e, extra_f)
WHERE q.prompt = seed.prompt AND q.question_type = 'MCQ_SINGLE';
